# FastAPI Miscellaneous Best Practices

## REST API Design

### Follow REST Principles
- Use appropriate HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, PATCH)
- Use meaningful resource URLs
- Return appropriate HTTP status codes
- Implement consistent error responses

### Status Codes
```python
from fastapi import status

# Use explicit status codes
@router.post("/users", status_code=status.HTTP_201_CREATED)
@router.get("/users/{user_id}", status_code=status.HTTP_200_OK)
@router.delete("/users/{user_id}", status_code=status.HTTP_204_NO_CONTENT)
```

## Response Serialization

### Leverage FastAPI's Built-in Serialization
- Use `response_model` parameter for automatic serialization
- Let FastAPI handle JSON conversion
- Don't manually serialize Pydantic models to dict

```python
@router.get("/users/{user_id}", response_model=UserResponse)
async def get_user(user_id: int):
    user = await get_user_by_id(user_id)
    return user  # FastAPI handles serialization automatically
```

## Documentation Best Practices

### Environment-Based Documentation
```python
from fastapi import FastAPI

SHOW_DOCS_ENVIRONMENT = ("local", "staging")

app_configs = {"title": "My Cool API"}
if ENVIRONMENT not in SHOW_DOCS_ENVIRONMENT:
    app_configs["openapi_url"] = None  # Hide docs in production

app = FastAPI(**app_configs)
```

### Rich API Documentation
```python
@router.post(
    "/posts",
    response_model=PostResponse,
    status_code=status.HTTP_201_CREATED,
    description="Create a new blog post",
    tags=["Posts"],
    summary="Create Post",
    responses={
        status.HTTP_201_CREATED: {
            "model": PostResponse,
            "description": "Post created successfully",
        },
        status.HTTP_400_BAD_REQUEST: {
            "model": ErrorResponse,
            "description": "Invalid post data",
        },
    },
)
async def create_post(post_data: PostCreate):
    pass
```

## Testing Setup

### Async Test Client from Day One
```python
import pytest
from httpx import AsyncClient
from fastapi.testclient import TestClient

@pytest.fixture
async def client():
    async with AsyncClient(app=app, base_url="http://test") as client:
        yield client

@pytest.mark.asyncio
async def test_create_post(client: AsyncClient):
    response = await client.post("/posts", json={"title": "Test"})
    assert response.status_code == 201
```

